C = [
("h1", "14. The Two Passages Side by Side"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 5:12–21 and 1 Corinthians 15:21–22 and 45–49 share two names, one enemy and one final title, and they differ in the question they answer, the words they use and the way they describe those who belong to each man. The thesis of the volume is that these differences are not accidents of style. They follow from the question each passage is answering."),
("sub", "14.1 The questions"),
("p", "A passage is best read by asking what it is for. Romans 5:12–21 is placed after the argument that all men are under sin and that the righteousness of God is given freely. Its question is how sin, death and righteousness came to men. First Corinthians 15:21–22 and 45–49 are placed in a chapter that answers the denial that the dead rise. Their question is how the dead are raised and with what body. The table sets the two side by side."),
("ctbl", "The two passages compared.", [
    ("Feature", "Romans 5:12–21", "1 Corinthians 15:21–22, 45–49"),
    ("The question", "How sin, death and righteousness reached “all men”", "How the dead are raised, and with what body they come"),
    ("The letter’s subject at that point", "Justification, and the results of being justified", "The resurrection of the dead, and the denial of it"),
    ("Names for the first man", "“one man,” “Adam,” “one that sinned”", "“Adam,” “the first man Adam,” “the first man,” “the earthy”"),
    ("Names for the second man", "“him that was to come,” “one man, Jesus Christ,” “one”", "“Christ,” “the last Adam,” “the second man,” “the Lord from heaven,” “the heavenly”"),
    ("Words for what the first man did", "“offence,” “transgression,” “disobedience,” “sinned”", "“was made a living soul”; “of the earth, earthy”"),
    ("Words for what the second man did", "“righteousness,” “obedience,” “free gift”", "“was made a quickening spirit”; “the Lord from heaven”"),
    ("What reaches those joined to each", "Condemnation and justification of life; sin and righteousness", "Death and being made alive; the image of the earthy and the image of the heavenly"),
    ("The joining word", "“by” (“by one man,” “by the offence of one,” “by the righteousness of one”)", "“in” (“in Adam,” “in Christ”)"),
    ("The scope words", "“all men,” “many,” and “they which receive”", "“all,” each bounded by “in Adam” or “in Christ”; “they that are Christ’s” (15:23)"),
    ("The final title", "“Jesus Christ our Lord”", "“our Lord Jesus Christ”"),
  ], [0.22, 0.39, 0.39]),
("sub", "14.2 Where they meet"),
("p", "The passages are not unrelated. They meet at four points. The first is the two names. Both passages set Adam beside Christ, and both set one man’s result beside another man’s. The second is the enemy. Death reigns in Romans 5 and is the last enemy to be destroyed in 1 Corinthians 15. The third is the trio of sin, death and the law. They are named in Romans 5:12–14 and 20, and they are named again in 1 Corinthians 15:56. The fourth is the close. Both end on the name of the Lord, in the same words reordered: “by Jesus Christ our Lord” and “through our Lord Jesus Christ.”"),
("p", "These agreements show one writer at work. They do not show that the two passages are one passage. A man who speaks twice of the same enemy, and twice of the same two persons, may be answering different questions, and the evidence that he is lies in the words each time."),
("sub", "14.3 Where they differ"),
("p", "The differences are of three kinds. The first is the question, which is set out above. The second is the kind of thing said of the heads. In Romans 5 the first man does an act: he offends, transgresses, disobeys. The second man does an act: he is righteous, he obeys, he gives. In 1 Corinthians 15 the first man is something: a living soul, earthy, of the earth. The second man is something: a quickening spirit, heavenly, from heaven. The two passages set the men against each other in different ways. One compares what they did, and the other compares what they are."),
("p", "The third difference is the scope. In Romans 5 the question is how far the “all men” of the second half reaches, and the reader is directed to verse 17 and its clause of reception. In 1 Corinthians 15 the scope is carried by the preposition: the “all” of each half is the “all” who are in the man named. The passages do not contradict each other. They state the same kind of limit in different places and by different means."),
("sub", "14.4 Two ways of misreading"),
("p", "Two misreadings follow from taking the passages for one. The first reads Romans 5 as if it were about the resurrection body. It is not. The passage does not mention the resurrection of the dead, or a body, or an image. A reader who hears the second man’s result as //being made alive// in the sense of 1 Corinthians 15:22 has carried the language of one passage into the other."),
("p", "The second misreading reads 1 Corinthians 15:22 as if it carried the whole argument of Romans 5. “In Adam all die” is a short sentence. It says that all die in Adam, and in the chapter it is used for a single purpose, to show that the resurrection of the dead is to be expected since death came by a man. A reader who builds a full doctrine of guilt and of imputation from the half-sentence “in Adam all die” is asking it to do what the sentence is not asked to do in its chapter. The next chapter returns to this point when it sets out what the passages do not say."),
("sub", "14.5 What neither passage names"),
("p", "One further observation belongs here. Neither passage names Israel. Neither passage names a covenant, or the seed of Abraham, or the nations as peoples. Romans 5 names Moses once, as a point in time, and names the law as the thing that entered. First Corinthians 15 names Cephas, the twelve, James and Paul as witnesses, and it names no covenant at all. The two heads, Adam and Christ, are not set in either passage over one nation or another. The volume does not say that Israel is outside the story told by the two men, and it does not say that Israel is inside it. It says that neither passage speaks of Israel’s covenants, and that a reader who finds them there has supplied the names."),
("p", "The point guards against a particular mistake. A reader who hears //two humanities// in the two passages may be tempted to identify them with two peoples. The passages do not do so. In Romans 5 the first group is “all men,” and the second is “they which receive.” In 1 Corinthians 15 the first is “they also that are earthy,” and the second is “they also that are heavenly.” These are the descriptions the passages give, and the volume holds to them."),
]
